1. Viewport Testing
* Narrow viewport (Mobile)
I verified that the .green-nav successfully shifts from a horizontal layout to 
a hamburger vertical flex stack. Gallery and feature cards also collapse into a single-column layout
using the auto-fit and minimax() grid settings to prevent any clipping or overflows

* Medium viewport (Tablet)
Cards turned into multi-column grids as expected. PAdding scales fluidly using the custom properties i applied
as well as spacing.

* Wide viewport (Desktop)
The main container successfully adheres to the max-inline-size: 1200px constraint rule I applied. This makes the content
center nice and neatly on the page with balanced spacing.

2. Zoom testing
* I zoomed the browser (firefox) to the max 200% zoom settings. I observed that the page layout reflows neatly without breaking text boundaries or overlapping elements
The properties (inline-size and block-size) perfectly allow the containers to expand naturally without text scaling and none of the contents within clipping.

3. Keyboard focus and accessibility
* I navigated through interactive elements such as navigation links, cards and buttons using only the TAB key. I verified that all interactive elements
received a clear indicator (:focus-visible) which is defined in the states layer. Focus order works as intended, starting from top to bottom, left to right!

4. Preference and fallback testing
* I tested the reduced-motion preference query by toggling the system-reduced-motion setting and verified that motion was reduced.
* In addition to testing reduced motion, I tested the layout fallback by changing the @supports condition to simulate an unsupported browser. things like .feature-card
defaulted to a standard block layout